// Pull request author: frack113
// Change: update to existing rule
// Title: Boot Configuration Tampering Via Bcdedit.EXE
// Sigma ID: 1444443e-6757-43e4-9ea4-c8fc705f79a2
// Status: stable | Level: high
// Author: E.M. Anhaus (originally from Atomic Blue Detections, Endgame), oscd.community
// Date: 2019-10-24 | Modified: 2023-02-15
// ATT&CK: impact, T1490
// Description: Detects the use of the bcdedit command to tamper with the boot configuration data. This
//   technique is often times used by malware or attackers as a destructive way before launching
//   ransomware.
// False positives:
//   - Unlikely
// References:
//   - https://github.com/redcanaryco/atomic-red-team/blob/f339e7da7d05f6057fdfcdd3742bfcf365fee2a9/atomics/T1490/T1490.md
//   - https://eqllib.readthedocs.io/en/latest/analytics/c4732632-9c1d-4980-9fa8-1d98c93f918e.html
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/ad147b5514596e70f2782769d9833046b7c8e12b/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_bcdedit_boot_conf_tamper.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (FolderPath endswith "\\bcdedit.exe" or ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName =~ "bcdedit.exe") and ProcessCommandLine contains "set" and ((ProcessCommandLine contains "bootstatuspolicy" and ProcessCommandLine contains "ignoreallfailures") or (ProcessCommandLine contains "recoveryenabled" and ProcessCommandLine contains "no"))
